ta.gov/unemploy/claims_arch.asp\",\"https://www.dol.gov/ui/data.pdf\",\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/ICSA\"],\"runAt\":\"2026-06-16T12:33:22Z\",\"reasoning\":[{\"kind\":\"heading\",\"text\":\"Forecast for DOL initial claims, week ending June 13, 2026\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"The resolver is the first official DOL ETA Unemployment Insurance Weekly Claims advance seasonally adjusted initial claims figure for the week-ending date June 13, 2026, not any later revised history.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Opened the DOL ETA weekly claims archive and publication schedule page.\",\"result\":\"Official schedule says the UI Weekly Claims News Release is published each week on Thursday morning at 8:30 AM EST, lists 1 exception date in 2026, Wednesday November 25, 2026, and the page was updated June 11, 2026.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Opened the current official DOL UI Weekly Claims PDF release dated June 11, 2026.\",\"result\":\"For week ending June 6, 2026, seasonally adjusted initial claims were 229000, up 4000 from the prior unrevised 225000; the four-week moving average was 219000, up 4250.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Read the DOL release table for recent and prior-year claims context.\",\"result\":\"The DOL table shows SA initial claims of 229000 for June 6, 2026, 225000 for May 30, 2026, 212000 for May 23, 2026, and 246000 for the comparable prior-year week.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"history.lookup\",\"call\":\"Opened FRED ICSA as a history mirror for the same ETA source series.\",\"result\":\"FRED showed 2026-06-06 at 229000, 2026-05-30 at 225000, 2026-05-23 at 212000, 2026-05-16 at 210000, 2026-05-09 at 212000, and next release date Jun 18, 2026.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Base-rate/reference class: for weekly initial claims, the near-term reference class is adjacent weekly prints around the latest release; the last five fetched SA observations are 212000, 210000, 212000, 225000, and 229000, while the official four-week average is 219000.\"},{\"kind\":\"math\",\"text\":\"Blend latest level 229000, prior week 225000, and four-week average 219000 with more weight on persistence: 0.40*229000 + 0.35*225000 + 0.25*219000 = 225100, rounded to the nearest 1000 gives 225000. Set an 80% interval of roughly +/-16000 around the rounded point, giving 209000 to 243000.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Counter-consideration: the latest unadjusted claims rose sharply by 39713 to 228276, and seasonal adjustment expected a large increase of 35595, so a second high print is plausible; however, the SA series often mean-reverts after jumps and the latest 229000 is already above the 219000 four-week average.\"},{\"kind\":\"forecast\",\"point\":225000,\"ciLow\":209000,\"ciHigh\":243000}]}\nrecords/brier-judges/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t23-38-10z-reasoning-judge/codex_last_message.txt:1:{\"schemaVersion\":\"brier_reasoning_judge_v1\",\"summary\":\"Across the six traces, Brier usually found official sources, named the resolving series, incorporated pre-submit review findings, and converted draft weaknesses into clearer resolver language, explicit priors, and interval math. The repeated weakness is that fast public-release runs often repair missing structure late in the trace with judgmental assumptions rather than fetching or representing the most relevant historical sequence, release calendar, or formula inputs in a reusable way. Several targets are mechanically resolvable but differ in character: some are public-release nowcasts needing source adapters and scheduled release metadata, while the CTC target is closer to a statutory formula nowcast and should be handled by a strategy/formula pack rather than the generic fast prompt alone.\",\"reviewedRunCount\":6,\"strengths\":[\"All six runs use official or near-official source families for the final resolver rather than news or secondary summaries.\",\"Pre-submit review is materially useful: blocking resolver, prior, and interval findings are reflected in the final reasoning for every run.\",\"Most final traces distinguish the target from nearby lookalikes, such as MTS refunds versus IRS filing-season refunds, CTC maximum versus refundable portion, and ONS LFS unemployment versus payroll or claimant-count indicators.\",\"Several runs include transparent arithmetic for the point estimate and interval, making audit failures easier to diagnose.\",\"The ONS, SNAP, IRS refunds, and MTS traces correctly use release timing or first-print concepts rather than later revised values.\"],\"systemIssues\":[{\"issueId\":\"historical_sequence_not_structured\",\"severity\":\"high\",\"area\":\"prompt\",\"summary\":\"Runs frequently acknowledge the need for recent same-series history but either do not fetch a clean sequence or encode it only as prose, leaving priors and intervals partly judgmental.\",\"evidenceRunSlugs\":[\"medicaid-chip-enrollment-april-2026\",\"snap-max-allotment-family-4-fy2027\",\"individual-income-tax-refunds-fy2026\",\"uk-unemployment-rate-jul-sep-2026\"]},{\"issueId\":\"resolver_date_repairs_are_repeated\",\"severity\":\"high\",\"area\":\"validator\",\"summary\":\"Multiple drafts had resolver date/source problems that were caught only by review; the system should validate inferred cadence, placeholder URLs, and input-date versus resolving-publication mismatches before draft submission.\",\"evidenceRunSlugs\":[\"irs-total-refunds-october-2026\",\"ctc-maximum-per-child-ty2027\",\"snap-max-allotment-family-4-fy2027\"]},{\"issueId\":\"formula_targets_need_separate_strategy\",\"severity\":\"medium\",\"area\":\"pack\",\"summary\":\"The CTC target is dominated by statute, chained-CPI windows, and rounding thresholds, which does not fit the generic fast public-release forecasting pattern as well as a formula-nowcast strategy would.\",\"evidenceRunSlugs\":[\"ctc-maximum-per-child-ty2027\"]},{\"issueId\":\"interval_calibration_afterthought\",\"severity\":\"medium\",\"area\":\"prompt\",\"summary\":\"Intervals are often justified after the fact with plausible but ad hoc assumptions rather than a standard interval object tied to observed volatility, revision error, sampling error, or discrete probability mass.\",\"evidenceRunSlugs\":[\"medicaid-chip-enrollment-april-2026\",\"snap-max-allotment-family-4-fy2027\",\"individual-income-tax-refunds-fy2026\",\"irs-total-refunds-october-2026\",\"ctc-maximum-per-child-ty2027\",\"uk-unemployment-rate-jul-sep-2026\"]},{\"issueId\":\"prior_catalog_reconciliation_untyped\",\"severity\":\"medium\",\"area\":\"ui\",\"summary\":\"When catalog priors or prior runs are used, the trace does not expose a structured prior-versus-update path; reconciliation appears as prose, making run comparison and audit harder.\",\"evidenceRunSlugs\":[\"snap-max-allotment-family-4-fy2027\",\"irs-total-refunds-october-2026\",\"ctc-maximum-per-child-ty2027\"]},{\"issueId\":\"release_calendar_not_adapter_backed\",\"severity\":\"medium\",\"area\":\"data_adapter\",\"summary\":\"Release dates are sometimes verified manually in traces, but the same source families need reusable calendar adapters so the runner can distinguish official scheduled dates, expected windows, and unknown future first-print pages.\",\"evidenceRunSlugs\":[\"individual-income-tax-refunds-fy2026\",\"irs-total-refunds-october-2026\",\"ctc-maximum-per-child-ty2027\",\"uk-unemployment-rate-jul-sep-2026\"]}],\"recommendations\":[{\"priority\":\"now\",\"area\":\"validator\",\"summary\":\"Add resolver-date/source validation for future first-print targets.\",\"rationale\":\"Several traces needed review to catch unsupported resolution dates, stale analogue URLs, or confusion between formula input dates and final resolving publications. Catching this before finalization improves auditability and reduces bad catalog metadata.\",\"proposedChange\":\"Require each cell to classify resolutionDate as official_scheduled_release, official_placeholder_release, policy_deadline, or unresolved_until_first_post. Reject or warn when the source URL is a prior-year analogue without a stable index/placeholder, or when the date source is an input release rather than the resolving publication.\",\"expectedImpact\":\"Fewer cadence-inferred dates and fewer future targets wired to stale resolver URLs.\",\"risk\":\"Some valid targets may lack official calendars and need explicit unresolved-until-first-post handling rather than a hard failure.\",\"evidenceRunSlugs\":[\"irs-total-refunds-october-2026\",\"ctc-maximum-per-child-ty2027\",\"snap-max-allotment-family-4-fy2027\"]},{\"priority\":\"now\",\"area\":\"prompt\",\"summary\":\"Require a structured prior/update/interval block in every forecast trace.\",\"rationale\":\"The traces often contain the right ingredients but mix them into prose. A structured representation would make weaknesses visible to validators, comparisons, and future packs.\",\"proposedChange\":\"Add required fields or reasoning sections: modelPrior.value, modelPrior.method, historicalSample.values, adjustment.components, interval.method, interval.inputs, and interval.impliedBounds. Allow 'not fetched' only with a reason and a fallback uncertainty rule.\",\"expectedImpact\":\"More consistent priors and intervals, and easier detection of hand-waved adjustments.\",\"risk\":\"Fast runs may take longer or produce more validation failures until adapters provide the needed history automatically.\",\"evidenceRunSlugs\":[\"medicaid-chip-enrollment-april-2026\",\"individual-income-tax-refunds-fy2026\",\"uk-unemployment-rate-jul-sep-2026\"]},{\"priority\":\"next\",\"area\":\"data_adapter\",\"summary\":\"Build official history adapters for repeated source families.\",\"rationale\":\"Medicaid, MTS, IRS filing statistics, ONS MGSX, SNAP COLA, and CPI-linked tax parameters recur and need consistent historical samples rather than one-off page reads.\",\"proposedChange\":\"Create adapters that return typed recent observations, first-print dates, units, revision/vintage flags, and source URLs for CMS Medicaid enrollment, Treasury MTS Table 4, IRS Filing Season Statistics, ONS time series, USDA FNS SNAP COLA tables, and BLS CPI/C-CPI-U release windows.\",\"expectedImpact\":\"Better model priors, less manual arithmetic, and fewer source-selection mistakes.\",\"risk\":\"Adapters can encode wrong table mappings if not tested against historical first prints.\",\"evidenceRunSlugs\":[\"medicaid-chip-enrollment-april-2026\",\"individual-income-tax-refunds-fy2026\",\"irs-total-refunds-october-2026\",\"uk-unemployment-rate-jul-sep-2026\"]},{\"priority\":\"next\",\"area\":\"pack\",\"summary\":\"Add a formula-nowcast pack for statutory rounded-dollar targets.\",\"rationale\":\"The CTC run is mostly a threshold formula with discrete outcomes, not a generic trend forecast. A dedicated pack would push the agent to enumerate inputs, thresholds, remaining unknown months, and probability mass by rounded outcome.\",\"proposedChange\":\"Create a pack requiring: statutory base amount, indexing formula, base year, inflation window, observed input months, remaining input months, rounding rule, threshold table, and probability distribution over official rounded values.\",\"expectedImpact\":\"Cleaner forecasts for tax parameters, benefit formulas, and other indexed statutory values.\",\"risk\":\"Overconfidence if the pack treats legal interpretation as fixed when IRS implementation could differ.\",\"evidenceRunSlugs\":[\"ctc-maximum-per-child-ty2027\"]},{\"priority\":\"next\",\"area\":\"ui\",\"summary\":\"Show strategy-track updates separately from final prose reasoning.\",\"rationale\":\"Prior traces and catalog priors are useful but not structured enough to compare how a new run moved the forecast.\",\"proposedChange\":\"In the UI and record schema, add a compact update table with rows for catalog prior, official latest level, model prior, inside-view adjustment, policy/formula adjustment, and final forecast. Store numeric deltas and cited evidence per row.\",\"expectedImpact\":\"Reviewers can quickly see whether updates are evidence-backed or judgmental.\",\"risk\":\"May add clutter unless the UI keeps the table compact and collapsible.\",\"evidenceRunSlugs\":[\"snap-max-allotment-family-4-fy2027\",\"irs-total-refunds-october-2026\",\"ctc-maximum-per-child-ty2027\"]},{\"priority\":\"later\",\"area\":\"scheduler\",\"summary\":\"Route target types to specialized prompt modes instead of sending all through the fast public-release prompt.\",\"rationale\":\"Fast mode works for many official-release forecasts but is less suitable for formula thresholds, unresolved future placeholders, and targets requiring exact vintage handling.\",\"proposedChange\":\"Add target metadata such as release_series, statutory_formula, vintage_revision, first_print_page, and survey_sampling. Use it to select public_release_fast, formula_nowcast, vintage_series, or survey_nowcast prompt modes.\",\"expectedImpact\":\"Fewer mismatched reasoning templates and more relevant evidence collection.\",\"risk\":\"Misclassification could send a simple target to an overly complex prompt.\",\"evidenceRunSlugs\":[\"ctc-maximum-per-child-ty2027\",\"medicaid-chip-enrollment-april-2026\",\"uk-unemployment-rate-jul-sep-2026\"]}],\"promptPatchCandidates\":[{\"file\":\"agents/thesis-analyst/system.md\",\"patchSummary\":\"Add a required 'Structured forecast basis' section before the final forecast: latest official level, historical sample, model prior, evidence-backed adjustments, interval method, and explicit unresolved data gaps.\"},{\"file\":\"agents/thesis-analyst/system.md\",\"patchSummary\":\"Add resolver instructions: do not use an input release date as resolutionDate when the target resolves to a later agency publication; classify the date basis and cite the official calendar, placeholder, policy deadline, or first-post fallback.\"},{\"file\":\"agents/thesis-analyst/system.md\",\"patchSummary\":\"For formula-driven targets, require a threshold table and discrete probability mass over rounded outcomes; do not force a continuous trend model when the statutory formula dominates.\"},{\"file\":\"validators/forecast-cell validation surface\",\"patchSummary\":\"Warn when reasoning says a same-series history was not fetched but the interval uses realized-volatility language; require either historicalSample values or an explicit fallback uncertainty model.\"},{\"file\":\"validators/resolution metadata validation surface\",\"patchSummary\":\"Flag resolver URLs that point to prior-year analogue pages for future first-print targets unless a stable index or future placeholder is also present.\"}]}\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-01/2026-07-01t05-12-15z-cms-medicaid-pi-ex-parte-renewal-share-ky-aug-2026-august-2026-reporting-period/raw_response.txt:1:{\"slug\":\"medicaid-ex-parte-share-aug-2026-ky\",\"country\":\"US\",\"type\":\"data\",\"title\":\"Kentucky Medicaid ex parte renewal share, Aug. 2026\",\"question\":\"CMS State Medicaid and CHIP Eligibility Processing Data, Kentucky state row, August 2026 reporting period, original first-print submission: ex parte renewals as a share of completed Medicaid renewals.\",\"unit\":\"percent\",\"pointEstimate\":92.0,\"ciLow\":82.8,\"ciHigh\":98.6,\"confidence\":0.8,\"resolutionDate\":\"2026-12-15\",\"resolutionSource\":\"CMS, State Medicaid and CHIP Eligibility Processing Data (data.medicaid.gov)\",\"resolutionSourceUrl\":\"https://data.medicaid.gov/dataset/5abea2e0-3f8e-4b49-a50d-d63d5fd9103c\",\"resolutionRule\":\"Resolves to Kentucky's ex parte renewal share for the August 2026 reporting period, computed from the original (O) submission row in CMS dataset 5abea2e0-3f8e-4b49-a50d-d63d5fd9103c when CMS first publishes it. Numerator and denominator as published; share computed to one decimal percent. Use the first official CMS print available through data.medicaid.gov and ignore later updated submissions or revisions.\",\"dataPointId\":\"cms.medicaid_pi.ex_parte_renewal_share.ky.aug_2026\",\"historicalContext\":[{\"label\":\"KY 2025-07 original first-print ex parte renewal share\",\"value\":84.3},{\"label\":\"KY 2025-09 original first-print ex parte renewal share\",\"value\":88.2},{\"label\":\"KY 2025-11 original first-print ex parte renewal share\",\"value\":95.3},{\"label\":\"KY 2026-01 original first-print ex parte renewal share\",\"value\":89.6},{\"label\":\"KY 2026-02 latest inspected original first-print ex parte renewal share\",\"value\":89.9}],\"drivers\":[\"Kentucky's recent ex parte renewal share is already high, near 90 percent\",\"The observed series has a ceiling near 100 percent, limiting upside more than downside\",\"Renewal cohort composition can move numerator and denominator several percentage points month to month\",\"Eligibility data-match coverage and CMS renewal-policy compliance pressure support persistence at a high level\",\"Manual-heavy cohorts, reporting breaks, or data-source outages are the main downside risks\"],\"sourceContext\":[\"https://www.medicaid.gov/medicaid/national-medicaid-chip-program-information/medicaid-chip-enrollment-data/monthly-medicaid-chip-application-eligibility-determination-and-enrollment-reports-data\",\"https://data.medicaid.gov/dataset/5abea2e0-3f8e-4b49-a50d-d63d5fd9103c\",\"https://data.medicaid.gov/api/1/datastore/query/6165f45b-ca93-5bb5-9d06-db29c692a360/0\"],\"runAt\":\"2026-07-01T05:13:55Z\",\"reasoning\":[{\"kind\":\"heading\",\"text\":\"Forecast for Kentucky Medicaid ex parte renewal share, August 2026\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"The resolver is a Kentucky state row, not a national weighted average: the original first-publication August 2026 reporting-period row in CMS State Medicaid and CHIP Eligibility Processing Data. The target is the share of completed Medicaid renewals processed ex parte, reported in percent and rounded to one decimal.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked the CMS Medicaid.gov monthly reports page for the release vehicle and current update cadence.\",\"result\":\"Fetched official CMS monthly page evidence: Preliminary March 2026 Applications, Eligibility, and Enrollment Data was listed, and March 2026, February 2026, January 2026, December 2025, November 2025, October 2025, September 2025, August 2025, and July 2025 entries were Last Updated June 26, 2026; the page states data.Medicaid.gov is updated monthly.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked the data.medicaid.gov target dataset and API identifiers for the CMS eligibility-processing series.\",\"result\":\"Fetched CMS dataset id 5abea2e0-3f8e-4b49-a50d-d63d5fd9103c and datastore id 6165f45b-ca93-5bb5-9d06-db29c692a360; the target uses original submission status O, August 2026 reporting period, and a first-print resolution date of 2026-12-15 in the canonical ledger.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"repo.lookup\",\"call\":\"Read public generated official-source-derived Kentucky history for this exact CMS ex parte renewal-share series, excluding the existing catalog point and interval from evidence.\",\"result\":\"Fetched Kentucky original first-print shares: 2025-07 = 84.3 percent, 2025-09 = 88.2 percent, 2025-11 = 95.3 percent, 2026-01 = 89.6 percent, and 2026-02 = 89.9 percent.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"shell.math\",\"call\":\"Computed summary statistics from the five inspected Kentucky official-source-derived points.\",\"result\":\"Fetched calculated summaries: five-point mean = 89.5 percent, latest-three mean = 91.6 percent, adjacent changes = +3.9, +7.1, -5.7, and +0.3 percentage points, mean absolute adjacent change = 4.3 percentage points, and observed range = 11.0 percentage points.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Base-rate/reference-class anchor: the most relevant outside view is Kentucky's own recent original-submission first-print run for this CMS eligibility-processing series. The level is high and stable enough that latest-value persistence near 90 percent is a stronger prior than a national or multi-state average.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Level, momentum, and mechanism: Kentucky has already reached a high-ex-parte regime, with 2025-11 at 95.3 percent and February 2026 at 89.9 percent. Momentum is mildly positive from July 2025 to February 2026, but the January 2026 pullback shows the path is cohort-sensitive rather than a smooth trend to 100 percent.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Prior/update/interval: prior model is Kentucky latest-value persistence blended with the latest-three mean, using the five inspected original first-print points from 2025-07, 2025-09, 2025-11, 2026-01, and 2026-02. Starting from latest 89.9 percent, I add +1.3 pp for the latest-three mean being 91.6 percent, +0.8 pp for high-level persistence and CMS compliance pressure, and 0.0 pp for the ceiling-constrained trend after the January dip, giving 92.0 after rounding. The 80% interval starts from realized first-print dispersion of 11.0 pp and mean adjacent move of 4.3 pp, widened for missing March-August first prints and skewed downward because upside is capped near 100.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Counter-consideration: downside outside the interval would require a manual-heavy renewal cohort, data-source outage, eligibility-system issue, or reporting break pushing Kentucky below 82.8 percent. Upside outside the interval would require nearly universal ex parte processing and favorable cohort mix pushing the first print above 98.6 percent despite the natural ceiling.\"},{\"kind\":\"math\",\"text\":\"Point calculation: 89.9 latest value + 1.3 pp pull toward latest-three mean of 91.6 + 0.8 pp high-level persistence/compliance adjustment = 92.0 percent after one-decimal rounding. Interval calculation: use a lower half-width of 9.2 pp and upper half-width of 6.6 pp, reflecting the 11.0 pp observed range, 4.3 pp mean absolute move, six-month missing-data horizon, and 100 percent ceiling; 92.0 - 9.2 = 82.8 and 92.0 + 6.6 = 98.6.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Resolution-date note: the official CMS page verifies the monthly public data.Medicaid.gov release vehicle and current update pattern, while the canonical ledger target supplies 2026-12-15 for this August 2026 fixed-vintage row. I did not find a separate CMS future-dated placeholder for the exact Kentucky August 2026 row, so the forecast remains tied to the ledger date and resolves on the first official CMS dataset print.\"},{\"kind\":\"forecast\",\"point\":92.0,\"ciLow\":82.8,\"ciHigh\":98.6}]}\nrecords/2026-06-23/live/spm-child-poverty-2025.json:1:{\"distribution\":{\"format\":\"numeric_cdf_v1\",\"pointCount\":201,\"support\":{\"lower\":10.25,\"upper\":16.25},\"points\":[{\"value\":10.25,\"probability\":0},{\"value\":10.28,\"probability\":0.00181818181818},{\"value\":10.31,\"probability\":0.00363636363636},{\"value\":10.34,\"probability\":0.00545454545455},{\"value\":10.37,\"probability\":0.00727272727273},{\"value\":10.4,\"probability\":0.00909090909091},{\"value\":10.43,\"probability\":0.0109090909091},{\"value\":10.46,\"probability\":0.0127272727273},{\"value\":10.49,\"probability\":0.0145454545455},{\"value\":10.52,\"probability\":0.0163636363636},{\"value\":10.55,\"probability\":0.0181818181818},{\"value\":10.58,\"probability\":0.02},{\"value\":10.61,\"probability\":0.0218181818182},{\"value\":10.64,\"probability\":0.0236363636364},{\"value\":10.67,\"probability\":0.0254545454545},{\"value\":10.7,\"probability\":0.0272727272727},{\"value\":10.73,\"probability\":0.0290909090909},{\"value\":10.76,\"probability\":0.0309090909091},{\"value\":10.79,\"probability\":0.0327272727273},{\"value\":10.82,\"probability\":0.0345454545455},{\"value\":10.85,\"probability\":0.0363636363636},{\"value\":10.88,\"probability\":0.0381818181818},{\"value\":10.91,\"probability\":0.04},{\"value\":10.94,\"probability\":0.0418181818182},{\"value\":10.97,\"probability\":0.0436363636364},{\"value\":11,\"probability\":0.0454545454545},{\"v