at 4.3.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"The main upside risk is that the household survey can be noisy and BLS says the unemployment-rate monthly-change confidence interval is about plus or minus 0.3 percentage point near a 6.0 percent rate; a softer June household survey could round to 4.4 or 4.5 even if payrolls remain positive.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"The main downside risk is continued strong labor demand: May payrolls rose 172,000, participation was steady at 61.8 percent, and the unemployment rate has held at 4.3 despite month-to-month changes in unemployment levels.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"math\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Use persistence as the anchor: median of Jan-May rounded rates = 4.3. Apply no drift because May payrolls were strong and participation was flat, but widen the 80 percent interval to roughly one BLS household-survey rounded-rate move below and above: 4.1 to 4.6.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"forecast\\\",\\\"point\\\":4.3,\\\"ciLow\\\":4.1,\\\"ciHigh\\\":4.6}]}\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-16z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/cells.with_activity.json:79:        \\\"result\\\": \\\"Seasonally adjusted unemployment rates in table A-1 were Jan 2026 4.3, Feb 2026 4.4, Mar 2026 4.3, Apr 2026 4.3, May 2026 4.3 percent.\\\"\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-16z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/cells.with_activity.json:85:        \\\"result\\\": \\\"FRED showed May 2026 4.3, Apr 2026 4.3, Mar 2026 4.3, Feb 2026 4.4, Jan 2026 4.3, updated Jun 5, 2026, with next release date Jul 2, 2026.\\\"\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-16z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/parsed_cells.json:79:        \\\"result\\\": \\\"Seasonally adjusted unemployment rates in table A-1 were Jan 2026 4.3, Feb 2026 4.4, Mar 2026 4.3, Apr 2026 4.3, May 2026 4.3 percent.\\\"\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-16z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/parsed_cells.json:85:     