 prior: no formal time-series model was fit for this fast run; the prior is persistence around the recent trend level with uncertainty sized from recent first-print volatility, because monthly approvals are lumpy and the four-month reference sample is small.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Level and momentum split: the April level of 16,710 was about 3.8% below the April trend level of 17,363, so I allow a modest bounce. Momentum is negative over March-April, so the bounce should be smaller than the February spike.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Mechanism split: detached houses were comparatively stable while multi-unit approvals drove the big 2026 swings. Policy pressure to lift housing supply is an upside force, but financing, construction costs, and project feasibility keep the near-term print noisy rather than persistently strong.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"math\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Point: start from a flat-to-slightly-positive base rate near +0.5%, add about +2.0 percentage points for mean reversion from April being 3.8% below trend, subtract about -0.5 percentage points for weak March-April momentum, giving +2.0%. Interval: recent monthly changes span roughly -10.5% to +29.7%; for an 80% first-print interval, use about -12/+14 percentage points around the point despite the small four-month sample, skewed upward for apartment-project lumpiness, giving -10.0% to +16.0%.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Counter-consideration and scenarios: downside outside the interval would be another large apartment-project drop or delayed approvals pushing the print below -10.0%; upside outside the interval would be a renewed multi-unit approval surge like February, above +16.0%; the central case is a partial rebound with no repeat of February's one-off jump.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Review disposition: accepted the critique to clarify the persistence/recent-volatility model prior, replace the unstable late