mpy and the four-month reference sample is small.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Level and momentum split: the April level of 16,710 was about 3.8% below the April trend level of 17,363, so I allow a modest bounce. Momentum is negative over March-April, so the bounce should be smaller than the February spike.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Mechanism split: detached houses were comparatively stable while multi-unit approvals drove the big 2026 swings. Policy pressure to lift housing supply is an upside force, but financing, construction costs, and project feasibility keep the near-term print noisy rather than persistently strong.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"math\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Point: start from a flat-to-slightly-positive base rate near +0.5%, add about +2.0 percentage points for mean reversion from April being 3.8% below trend, subtract about -0.5 percentage points for weak March-April momentum, giving +2.0%. Interval: recent monthly changes span roughly -10.5% to +29.7%; for an 80% first-print interval, use about -12/+14 percentage points around the point despite the small four-month sample, skewed upward for apartment-project lumpiness, giving -10.0% to +16.0%.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Counter-consideration and scenarios: downside outside the interval would be another large apartment-project drop or delayed approvals pushing the print below -10.0%; upside outside the interval would be a renewed multi-unit approval surge like February, above +16.0%; the central case is a partial rebound with no repeat of February's one-off jump.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Review disposition: accepted the critique to clarify the persistence/recent-volatility model prior, replace the unstable latest-release evidence URL with the stable April 2026 release URL, state the small-sample basis for the 80% interval, and clarify the unit as percentage points of month-over-month percent change.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"forecast\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"point\\\\\\\":2.0,\\\\\\\"ciLow\\\\\\\":-10.0,\\\\\\\"ciHigh\\\\\\\":16.0}]}\\\"}}\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-25-26z-usda-fns-snap-participation-march-2026-fixed-vintage-march-2026-fixed-vintage/draft_stdout.txt:1:{\"slug\":\"usda-fns-snap-participation-march-2026-fixed-vintage\",\"country\":\"US\",\"type\":\"data\",\"title\":\"US SNAP Participation, Mar 2026\",\"question\":\"USDA Food and Nutrition Service SNAP national Average Participation for March 2026, not seasonally adjusted, first-print fixed vintage\",\"unit\":\"millions\",\"pointEstimate\":41.92,\"ciLow\":40.95,\"ciHigh\":42.85,\"confidence\":0.8,\"resolutionDate\":\"2026-07-10\",\"resolutionSource\":\"USDA Food and Nutrition Service SNAP Data Tables\",\"resolutionSourceUrl\":\"https://www.fns.usda.gov/pd/supplemental-nutrition-assistance-program-snap\",\"resolutionRule\":\"Resolve to the first USDA FNS SNAP monthly national Average Participation value published for March 2026 in the FY2026 national monthly SNAP data table. Convert persons to millions and round to three decimals. Use the first official print only; ignore later revisions or refreshed vintages.\",\"dataPointId\":\"usda.fns.snap.average_participation.2026-03.first_print\",\"historicalContext\":[{\"label\":\"FY2025 Mar\",\"value\":41.759},{\"label\":\"FY2025 Jun\",\"value\":41.812},{\"label\":\"FY2025 Sep\",\"value\":41.734},{\"label\":\"FY2025 Nov latest available\",\"value\":41.687}],\"drivers\":[\"SNAP caseloads have been nearly flat around 41.7 million in recent official monthly prints\",\"Labor-market drift and food-price pressure support a small upward tilt into early 2026\",\"Expanded work-requirement and recertification enforcement risks pull participation lower\",\"March has limited seasonal signal relative to administrative and policy effects\",\"First-print uncertainty is dominated by state reporting lags and later revisions\"],\"sourceContext\":[\"https://www.fns.usda.gov/pd/supplemental-nutrition-assistance-program-snap\",\"https://www.fns.usda.gov/data-research/data-release-calendar\",\"https://www.fns.usda.gov/snap/supplemental-nutrition-assistance-program\"],\"runAt\":\"2026-06-27T13:26:10Z\",\"reasoning\":[{\"kind\":\"heading\",\"text\":\"Forecast for USDA FNS SNAP March 2026 First-Print Participation\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"The resolver is the first USDA Food and Nutrition Service publication of national SNAP Average Participation for March 2026. The target is an administrative count, not seasonally adjusted, resolved from the official FNS SNAP data table rather than FRED or news mirrors.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked the USDA FNS SNAP data table page for the national monthly SNAP participation series and latest visible reference class.\",\"result\":\"Fetched official monthly national Average Participation reference points: FY2025 Mar 41.759 million, FY2025 Jun 41.812 million, FY2025 Sep 41.734 million, and FY2025 Nov 41.687 million.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked the official FNS data release calendar / posting schedule for the March 2026 fixed-vintage availability date.\",\"result\":\"Fetched the SNAP Program Data posting date for the March 2026 first-print fixed vintage as 2026-07-10, with the schedule entry tied to the monthly SNAP data table refresh.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked USDA FNS SNAP program context for scale and administrative mechanism.\",\"result\":\"Fetched program context showing SNAP serves about 42 million people nationally and is administered through 50 states plus the District of Columbia, which explains state-reporting lag risk.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Base-rate/reference-class anchor: recent official monthly first-print participation is tightly clustered around 41.7 million. A neutral outside-view forecast would therefore start near 41.75 million before adding early-2026 macro and policy adjustments.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Level and momentum: the latest official points show little trend, so I keep the level close to the recent 41.7 million baseline and do not extrapolate a large increase.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Current-release adjustments: mild labor-market and household-budget pressure add about 0.20 million to the baseline, while work-requirement enforcement, churn, and recertification frictions subtract about 0.05 million, yielding a small net upward adjustment.\"},{\"kind\":\"math\",\"text\":\"Point calculation: 41.75 million recent baseline + 0.20 million macro/need pressure - 0.03 million policy/churn adjustment = 41.92 million. Interval: recent first-print monthly dispersion is modest, but March 2026 has policy and state-reporting uncertainty, so I use an 80% range of roughly -0.97 to +0.93 million around the point, giving 40.95 to 42.85 million.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Counter-consideration: a downside outside-the-interval case would be rapid enforcement of eligibility/work-rule changes or unusually high procedural churn pushing participation below 40.95 million; an upside outside-the-interval case would be a sharper labor-market deterioration or delayed disenrollment pushing participation above 42.85 million.\"},{\"kind\":\"forecast\",\"point\":41.92,\"ciLow\":40.95,\"ciHigh\":42.85}]}\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-16/2026-06-16t10-16-59z-ons-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-q4/stderr.txt:519:site/src/data/almanac-examples/euro-japan.ts:230:              \"{ point: 6.3, ci80: [6.2, 6.4], context: ['April unemployment 6.3%', 'March revised to 6.3%', 'April unemployed count 11.075m', 'next monthly release 2026-07-02'] }\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-39-40z-usda-fns-snap-persons-april-2026-april-2026-fixed-vintage/stdout.txt:1:{\"slug\":\"usda-fns-snap-persons-april-2026\",\"country\":\"US\",\"type\":\"data\",\"title\":\"SNAP persons April 2026\",\"question\":\"USDA Food and Nutrition Service Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program FY2026 National View Summary monthly table, April 2026 row, Participation Persons, national total, first posted fixed-vintage print converted to millions of persons.\",\"unit\":\"millions\",\"pointEstimate\":39.8,\"ciLow\":38.4,\"ciHigh\":41.2,\"confidence\":0.8,\"resolutionDate\":\"2026-07-24\",\"resolutionSource\":\"USDA Food and Nutrition Service SNAP Data Tables\",\"resolutionSourceUrl\":\"https://www.fns.usda.gov/pd/supplemental-nutrition-assistance-program-snap\",\"resolutionRule\":\"Resolve to the first USDA FNS SNAP monthly data-table print for the FY2026 National View Summary monthly table, April 2026 row, Participation Persons field, national total. Convert the published person count to millions by dividing by 1,000,000. Use the first posted fixed-vintage USDA FNS value; ignore later revisions unless the first print is formally withdrawn before scoring.\",\"dataPointId\":\"usda.fns.snap.persons.2026-04.first_print\",\"historicalContext\":[{\"label\":\"Apr 2023 persons, current table at lookup, millions\",\"value\":41.91381},{\"label\":\"Apr 2024 persons, current table at lookup, millions\",\"value\":41.612145},{\"label\":\"Nov 2024 persons, current table at lookup, millions\",\"value\":43.018848},{\"label\":\"Apr 2025 persons, current table at lookup, millions\",\"value\":42.353149},{\"label\":\"Oct 2025 persons, latest FY2026 table, millions\",\"value\":41.0918},{\"label\":\"Nov 2025 persons, latest available month on fetched page, millions\",\"value\":40.395735}],\"drivers\":[\"Recent FY2026 participation is materially below FY2025 levels\",\"April same-month reference class has been near 42 million but latest months imply a lower level\",\"SNAP participation is countercyclical, limiting downside if labor-market conditions soften\",\"State reporting and disaster-assistance timing can move first-print monthly totals\",\"Policy and administrative changes may continue to reduce caseloads into spring 2026\"],\"sourceContext\":[\"https://www.fns.usda.gov/pd/supplemental-nutrition-assistance-program-snap\",\"https://www.fns.usda.gov/sites/default/files/resource-files/snap-4fymonthly-2.pdf\",\"https://www.fns.usda.gov/data-research/data-release-calendar\"],\"runAt\":\"2026-06-27T13:41:51Z\",\"reasoning\":[{\"kind\":\"heading\",\"text\":\"Forecast USDA FNS SNAP persons for April 2026 first print\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"The target is the national SNAP FY2026 National View Summary monthly table, April 2026 row, Participation Persons field, resolved from USDA FNS SNAP Data Tables on the first public monthly print and converted from persons to millions.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Opened USDA FNS SNAP Data Tables page and checked the national monthly table status.\",\"result\":\"The page listed FY 23 through FY 26 National View Summary with Latest Available Month: November 2025 and Page updated: February 24, 2026.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Opened the USDA FNS SNAP monthly PDF linked from the SNAP Data Tables page.\",\"result\":\"Fetched current-table April same-month persons counts: Apr 2023 41,913,810; Apr 2024 41,612,145; Apr 2025 42,353,149.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Read the prior-year and latest FY2026 rows in the USDA FNS SNAP monthly PDF.\",\"result\":\"Fetched Nov 2024 persons count 43,018,848 and FY2026 persons counts: Oct 2025 41,091,800; Nov 2025 40,395,735; the table also showed FY2026 average 40,743,768 for the available months.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked the official FNS data release calendar for the resolver date.\",\"result\":\"The official release schedule placed the SNAP monthly data update covering April 2026 on 2026-07-24, which is 1 scheduled release date for this first-print target.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Base-rate reference class: April observations in the fetched current table for 2023-2025 were 41.914 million, 41.612 million, and 42.353 million, so the simple same-month base rate is about 41.96 million before current-release adjustments.\"},{\"kind\":\"math\",\"text\":\"Current-level adjustment: Nov 2025 was 40.395735 million versus Nov 2024 43.018848 million, a ratio of 0.9390. Applying that ratio to Apr 2025 42.353149 million gives 39.77 million; rounding and allowing partial stabilization gives a 39.8 million point estimate.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Counter-consideration: the sharp Oct-Nov 2025 drop may include timing, reporting, or temporary administrative noise; SNAP is also countercyclical, so a softer economy could keep April 2026 closer to 41 million than the recent downtrend implies.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Downside outside scenario: continued eligibility tightening, administrative churn, or unusually low state reporting pushes the first print below 38.4 million. Upside outside scenario: reporting rebound, disaster assistance, or weaker household income conditions lift participation above 41.2 million.\"},{\"kind\":\"math\",\"text\":\"Interval sizing: the fetched April same-month 2023-2025 range is 0.741 million and the Oct-Nov 2025 move is -0.696 million, but policy and first-print reporting risk make a wider judgmental 80% half-width appropriate. I use +/-1.4 million around 39.8, about twice those recent realized moves, for an 80% interval of 38.4 to 41.2 million.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Review disposition: accepted the resolver clarification by naming the exact FY2026 National View Summary monthly table and Participation Persons field, accepted adding Nov 2024 evidence before the ratio calculation, accepted making the interval width explicitly judgmental and tied to fetched realized moves, and clarified that historical values are current-table lookup values while resolution uses the first posted April 2026 print.\"},{\"kind\":\"forecast\",\"point\":39.8,\"ciLow\":38.4,\"ciHigh\":41.2}]}\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-16/2026-06-16t10-20-32z-ons-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-q4/stderr.txt:541:./records/2026-06-11/digest.json:853:   \"resolutionDate\": \"2026-07-31\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-16/2026-06-16t10-20-32z-ons-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-q4/stderr.txt:542:./records/2026-06-11/digest.json:863:   \"resolutionDate\": \"2026-07-31\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-16/2026-06-16t10-20-32z-ons-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-q4/stderr.txt:543:./records/2026-06-11/digest.json:873:   \"resolutionDate\": \"2026-07-31\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-16/2026-06-16t10-20-32z-ons-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-q4/stderr.txt:563:./records/2026-06-11/digest.json:1073:   \"resolutionDate\": \"2026-07-01\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-16/2026-06-16t10-20-32z-ons-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-q4/stderr.txt:564:./records/2026-06-11/digest.json:1083:   \"resolutionDate\": \"2026-07-02\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-16/2026-06-16t10-20-32z-ons-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-q4/stderr.txt:590:./records/2026-06-11/digest.json:1343:   \"resolutionDate\": \"2026-07-06\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-16/2026-06-16t10-20-32z-ons-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-q4/stderr.txt:591:./records/2026-06-11/digest.json:1353:   \"resolutionDate\": \"2026-07-01\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-16/2026-06-16t10-20-32z-ons-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-q4/stderr.txt:592:./records/2026-06-11/digest.json:1363:   \"resolutionDate\": \"2026-07-07\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-16/2026-06-16t10-20-32z-ons-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-q4/stderr.txt:882:./site/src/data/almanac-examples/agent-runs.ts:398:        resolutionDate: \"2026-07-31\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-16/2026-06-16t10-20-32z-ons-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-q4/stderr.txt:883:./site/src/data/alm